{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Extended the no-snapshot probe so the containment consequence is executed rather than derived from source: after each restart the harness now isolates the node from every peer for one second and samples whether it ever campaigns, how many servers its configuration holds, and its term before and after. Local construction feedback on the two modes: the feature-enabled restart, whose configuration is empty, stayed a follower and never campaigned, while the control restart holding {A,B,C} reached Candidate — so the isolation is a real trigger and the empty configuration is what keeps the defective node from campaigning. Both retained their term, which is the pre-vote extension's behaviour (a candidate does not increment its term before a pre-vote succeeds), so the term is recorded as an operand and deliberately not asserted. The successor experiment is a revision of that child question's previous experiment (same investigation) with the isolation phase described in scope and prefix, one admission over the fixture (zero snapshots, running configuration, ten committed commands) and four comparisons: the feature-enabled node's configuration is empty and it does not campaign (expected holds), and the control's configuration has three servers and it does campaign (expected holds). Incomplete: that receipt and its interpretation, and the still-unexecuted half of the containment story — that a node with an empty configuration also skips the membership check when granting votes to a candidate it does not know.\"}"}}
